  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on variance. Variance measures the spread of outcomes around the expected value and reflects riskiness. For Lottery A: outcomes are 6000 with 25% probability and 0 with 75%. For Lottery B: outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%). We compute the variance for each lottery to assess which has higher variance. Variance = Σ [probability × (outcome - expected value)²]. First, compute expected values: EA = 0.25×6000 + 0.75×0 = 1500. EB = 0.25×4000 + 0.25×2000 + 0.5×0 = 1000 + 500 + 0 = 1500. Both lotteries have the same expected value (1500). Now compute variances: VA = 0.25×(6000−1500)² + 0.75×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(4500)² + 0.75×(1500)² = 0.25×20,250,000 + 0.75×2,250,000 = 5,062,500 + 1,687,500 = 6,750,000. VB = 0.25×(4000−1500)² + 0.25×(2000−1500)² + 0.5×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(2500)² + 0.25×(500)² + 0.5×(1500)² = 0.25×6,250,000 + 0.25×250,000 + 0.5×2,250,000 = 1,562,500 + 62,500 + 1,125,000 = 2,749,999.99 ≈ 2,750,000. Thus, VA (6,750,000) > VB (2,750,000). The decision reason prefers the lottery with larger variance, which is Lottery A. Therefore, the reason can be applied and clearly favors A over B.",
